
"Dr Mundo": Welcome back. Your observations have helped you understand how different Atomons behave.

Player: What will we investigate next?

"Dr Mundo": This time, we'll look deeper into the atoms themselves.

Player: Their electrons?

"Dr Mundo": Exactly! More specifically, we'll focus on the electrons in the outermost energy level. We call these valence electrons.

Player: Why are valence electrons important?

"Dr Mundo": Because they play an important role in how atoms interact and form chemical bonds.

"Dr Mundo": Many atoms become more stable when their outermost energy level is filled.

Player: Is that what the Octet Rule is about?

"Dr Mundo": Very good! The Octet Rule describes the tendency of many atoms to gain, lose, or share electrons to achieve eight valence electrons.

Player: So atoms can change their number of electrons or share them?

"Dr Mundo": Correct. Some atoms gain electrons, some lose electrons, and others share electrons.

"Dr Mundo": Remember, the Octet Rule is a useful general rule, not something that applies to every atom. Hydrogen and helium, for example, follow a duet arrangement.

Player: How can we see the difference between atoms with different numbers of valence electrons?

"Dr Mundo": I want you to investigate three Atomons.

"Dr Mundo": First, observe Neon. It has a complete outer shell.

"Dr Mundo": Next, investigate Sodium. It has one valence electron.

"Dr Mundo": Finally, observe Chlorine. It has seven valence electrons.

"Dr Mundo": Compare their electron arrangements and think about why their outermost electrons matter.

